# The two halves of the tenure machinery that the benchmark corpus keeps
# apart, in one program.
#
# `xs` is built INSIDE the bind, so it sits above the beat's mark, is copied
# into the carry on every lap, and from the second lap its elements have lived
# a lap and are promoted into a tenure block. `acc` is made BEFORE the first
# bind, so it sits below the mark, and the copy walk repairs it rather than
# copying it out. What goes into `acc` is an element OF `xs`, so a node below
# the mark comes to hold a pointer into tenure — and `k_survives_x` answers
# yes for such a pointer, which is exactly why `k_repaired_settle` leaves it
# where it is.
#
# runtime.c said nothing measured had reached that. This reaches it. The
# eleven benchmarks split into three that tenure and never repair (widebench,
# indexbench, scanbench) and two that repair and never tenure (encodebench,
# pendbench), and none that do both, which is why four earlier attempts
# missed: the shapes are disjoint in everything the corpus runs.
#
# Reaching it is harmless here. This comment used to say the reason was the fix
# kanso#1209 landed -- a beat whose result is a heap value hands its tenure
# blocks to the depth outside instead of freeing them, so the block outlives
# the read below -- and to claim that a change which stopped handing them up
# turned this fixture red. Neither held. The carry's deep copy runs before the
# hand up and reads the tenured bytes out, so nothing below the mark still
# points into tenure by the time the block moves; and `ten_blocks=1` beside
# `ten_frees=1` reads the same whether the block is handed up or freed here,
# because the hand up moves only where the free happens.
#
# What the fixture now pins is `ten_handups=1`, which goes to nought on that
# change. The block that the hand up really keeps alive belongs to
# an_inner_beat_opens_its_tenure_in_the_block_outside, not to this program.
import "std/io"
import "std/list"

fn wide n
  fat = (i -> "element {i} with enough bytes in it to be worth promoting")
  list/to_list (list/map (list/take list/naturals n) fat)

fn gathered n xs acc
  stepped (n - 1) xs (push acc xs[n])

fn stepped 0 _ acc
  acc

# Each step allocates a list it discards. A chain step leaves its value
# where it is while the region has not drifted far since the last stage, so
# without garbage to drift a chain of small steps never stages, never
# carries, and never promotes -- and this fixture's whole subject is what
# the promotion does. `drift` is demanded by the arithmetic, which is what
# makes the list real; `n + 40 - drift` is `n`.
fn stepped n xs acc
  drift = length (wide 40)
  io/write "" .> (_ -> gathered (n + 40 - drift) xs acc)

fn loaded acc
  xs = wide 400
  stepped (length xs) xs acc

fn read_back out
  io/write "kept {length out}, first {out[1]}, last {out[400]}\n"

fn begin acc0
  io/write "" .> (_ -> loaded acc0) .> read_back

pub play = begin []
